## Can I close this window and answer from another one?

Your coding agent has a question about which tests to run. You want to close the terminal window on your workstation and answer from your laptop after lunch. A **terminal multiplexer** such as tmux gives the agent a terminal you can reach from either place. You can fit several of these terminals into one desktop window, each in a rectangle called a **pane**. You might use the left pane for a shell and the right one for the agent. A background process on the workstation, the tmux **server**, holds a terminal open for each pane. You can close your view of the split and come back to the same programs. GNU Screen and Zellij share this design.

Start on the machine where you want the agent and its tests to run. You'll need tmux and your usual coding agent there. One machine is enough to try closing a desktop window and picking up in another. For the laptop handoff, choose a workstation you can reach over **SSH**, which lets you open a shell on a remote machine. On the workstation, open a desktop terminal outside tmux. Change to your project directory and run `tty`.

Write down the device name it prints, beside the word “desktop.” The diagrams use Linux names, starting with `/dev/pts/3` for the desktop terminal. Your numbers can differ, and on macOS you might see `/dev/ttys003`. The shell is using a **pseudo-terminal** (PTY), a software terminal with two connected ends. The name you wrote down identifies the shell's end. Your terminal app, such as Ghostty or iTerm2, holds the **master** end. Your shell uses the **program-facing** end, labeled “slave” in the diagrams. To send the letters of `tty` to your shell, the app wrote to the master. To display the result, it read the output coming back through that same end. [Linux PTY manual](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pty.7.html)

<figure>

![Without tmux the terminal app holds the PTY master. The shell, or Vim while the shell waits, uses the other end.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-stack-without-tmux.png)

<figcaption>Follow the black arrows down from the terminal window and the green arrows back up to the window. In the middle, the operating system's “line discipline” handles terminal settings, such as whether to send a copy of the characters you type back to the screen. At the bottom, the foreground program is the one you're typing into. If you launch Vim, your shell waits while you edit. Vim uses the same terminal the shell used for its prompt.</figcaption>
</figure>

An agent you start at this prompt inherits the shell's terminal, as Vim does in the drawing. Closing the desktop window makes the app let go of the master. Losing that connection, a **hang-up**, can end an interactive job. Even if the agent survives, typing in a fresh window gives your answer to the shell in that window. To get back to the agent, you need to keep both its program and its terminal available. Start tmux before launching the agent so the server can hold the master for it.

## The server holds the pane terminals

**1. Create a session.** In the desktop terminal where you ran `tty`, create a named tmux workspace, or **session**. Use the name `agent` below, or choose a fresh name if you have a session called `agent`. Use the same name each time you connect to the session. The key combinations in this walkthrough assume the default tmux shortcuts.

```sh
tmux new -s agent
```

The `tmux` command you started is the **client**. It starts the server if needed and requests a session named `agent`. To **attach** to that session, the client gives the server access to your desktop terminal over a **control socket**, a connection between processes on the workstation. The server can then read your keys from the terminal and draw the panes there. Your original shell waits for the client to finish. The prompt you see belongs to a new shell that the server started inside the session.

A session holds one or more **tmux windows**. Think of them as tabs within the session. Each tmux window has its own arrangement of panes, and you see the panes of whichever window you've selected. Look at the status line along the bottom of your desktop terminal. With the default settings you'll see `[agent]` and a `*` beside the name of the current tmux window. Its first pane takes up the space above that line. If you put a second tmux window in this session, you could switch to it and use that whole space, then switch back to the arrangement you left in the first one. [tmux Getting Started](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/wiki/Getting-Started#terminology)

**2. Identify the first pane.** At the new prompt, run `tty` and label the result “shell pane.” Suppose you got `/dev/pts/3` before starting tmux and `/dev/pts/4` inside it. Your desktop app still holds the master of the first PTY, the **outer terminal**. The tmux server opened the second PTY for the pane and holds its master. The pane shell writes its prompt on the program-facing end. You see that prompt on your desktop because the server reads the output at the pane master and writes a screen update to the outer terminal.

Your client gave the server access to the outer terminal by passing a **terminal handle** over the control socket. A handle gives the server access to an open device, in this case the program-facing end of `/dev/pts/3`. A client in a different desktop window can supply a handle for its own outer terminal. The server will have somewhere else to draw the prompt, and the shell can keep using `/dev/pts/4` through the switch. [tmux client source](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/client.c)

**3. Give the agent a pane.** The default tmux **prefix** is `Ctrl-b`. It tells tmux to treat the next key as a shortcut. With the pane shell on screen, press `Ctrl-b`, release both keys, then press `%` to split the current tmux window into two panes side by side. The shell you measured stays on the left. The server opens another PTY and starts a shell on the right. The right pane is now the **active pane**, the one that gets your ordinary keystrokes. Both panes belong to the window marked with the star in the status line.

At the right-hand prompt, run `tty` before launching anything. Label the result “agent pane,” `/dev/pts/5` in the drawing. You now have two prompts and two different device names inside the split. Starting a program at either prompt gives it the terminal for that pane, so choose the right-hand prompt for the agent and keep the left one free for shell commands.

**4. Leave a question on screen.** At the right-hand prompt, start your coding agent in the project as you would from a plain shell. Ask it, “Ask me which test command to run, then wait for my answer.” Leave its question open. The shell on the right waits for the agent, which uses the terminal you measured there. Find the two programs at the bottom of the drawing and trace their connections up to the server.

<figure>

![The terminal window connects through outer PTY /dev/pts/3 to the tmux server. The server keeps two screens and holds the pane PTY masters for Bash on /dev/pts/4 at left and the coding agent on /dev/pts/5 at right. A dashed control socket connects the client to the server at the side.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-stack-with-tmux.png)

<figcaption>The three names from <code>tty</code> go beside the boxes marked “slave.” From the top, <code>/dev/pts/3</code> serves the desktop window. Below the server, <code>/dev/pts/4</code> serves the shell on the left (Bash in the drawing), and <code>/dev/pts/5</code> serves the agent on the right. The dashed line shows where the client passes its terminal handle at attach. Follow the solid arrows for keys and output. The server keeps a screen in memory for each pane, shown as two small grids above the pane terminals.</figcaption>
</figure>

You can read the agent's question. To tmux, a program waiting for an answer and a program running a quiet test can leave the same text on screen. You can use the shell while the question sits in the other pane.

**5. Close the desktop window while a command runs.** In the split, press `Ctrl-b`, then Left Arrow to select the shell pane. At the left-hand prompt, type `sleep 30 && date`. Press Enter and close this desktop terminal window during the thirty-second pause. Wait for the full thirty seconds to pass before opening a replacement. On your return, look for a timestamp from the interval when the window was closed.

Closing the window disconnects its client. With no client showing it, the session is **detached**. On the workstation, the server still holds the masters for both panes. The left shell can start `date` at the end of the pause, and the agent on the right can wait for your answer through its own terminal. The shell has somewhere to print the timestamp. You'll see what the server kept from both panes when you connect to the session again.

## How tmux redraws the split

Open a fresh desktop terminal of about the same size on the workstation, under the same account. At its prompt outside tmux, run `tmux ls` to list the sessions on your server. Look for the entry for `agent`. It reports one window even though you left two panes. You can recognize the session by name from any project directory in this new shell. At the same prompt, **reattach** by connecting a new client to the existing session.

```sh
tmux attach -t agent
```

Look above the prompt on the left for the timestamp and across the divider for the agent's question. The timestamp tells you when `date` ran. At the left-hand prompt, run `tty`. Compare the result with the shell-pane name you wrote down. In the example it's still `/dev/pts/4`. The server held that pane's master open throughout the pause, so the shell could keep writing to its terminal after you closed the desktop window.

To answer from your laptop now, go to [What survives an SSH disconnect?](#what-survives-an-ssh-disconnect) with the left pane still selected. To answer here on the workstation, press `Ctrl-b`, then Right Arrow to select the agent. Give it the test command at the same prompt where it asked the question.

The new desktop window opened after `date` had finished. It can show you the timestamp because tmux does the work of a **terminal emulator** for each pane. A terminal emulator interprets a program's output and keeps track of what the screen should contain. While you were away, the server read the timestamp and the shell prompt from the left pane's master. It could update its picture of that pane without a desktop window in which to draw it. [tmux manual](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1#DESCRIPTION)

The server keeps a **screen grid** for each pane, rows and columns of character positions called **cells**. In the left grid, the timestamp occupies part of one row and the prompt starts on the next. In the right grid, the agent can start a line in column one of its own terminal. The server keeps styling with the characters. If the agent used green for a question, tmux has the information to draw those letters in green on a terminal that supports the color.

Along with text, programs send terminal instructions as **escape sequences**, groups of bytes that can start with the Escape character. A program can use one sequence to set a color and another to move its **cursor**, the position where the next character will appear. tmux applies those instructions inside the pane that produced them. The agent can move its cursor to the top left to redraw a menu while the shell keeps its cursor at the end of the prompt across the divider.

To **redraw** a window for a client that has attached, the server takes the current cells from both grids, places each pane in the layout and adds the divider. It writes cursor movements, styling instructions and characters to the new outer terminal. Your desktop app interprets those instructions and draws the split. Suppose the agent opened a menu and cleared it while you were away. The server applied both changes to the right grid as they arrived. On reattach, you see the question the agent left on screen. The agent can accept your answer without printing the question again.

Once you have the split on screen, the server can send a smaller update. Suppose the program on the right puts an X at row 2, column 5 of its pane. If tmux sent that cursor movement straight through to the desktop, the X would land on the left. The diagram gives the left pane six columns and the divider one, so the right pane starts at outer column 8. The program can use coordinates measured from its own top left. The server knows where to put that cell on the desktop.

<figure>

![A program puts X at row 2, column 5 of a six-column pane. With six columns on the left and one divider, X appears at row 2, column 12 on the desktop terminal.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/pane-screen-grid-update.png)

<figcaption>Count five cells starting at column 8 and the X falls at outer column 12. tmux can write the Escape byte followed by <code>[2;12H</code> to move the outer cursor to row 2, column 12, then write X. Your terminal app draws the letter there. The cells on the other side of the divider keep their contents.</figcaption>
</figure>

<details>
<summary>Optional diagram about reading status from a quiet pane</summary>

<figure>

![A monitoring tool can infer status from terminal activity or use a report from the agent. Silence alone cannot distinguish waiting for input from running a quiet command.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-what-tmux-sees.png)

<figcaption>The lines on the left show two ways a separate tool could get a status such as “blocked.” It could infer one from activity in the pane, or the agent could report that it needs an answer. A shell that marks where commands begin and end can supply the boundary markers in the top box. You used the text of the question itself to decide what to type.</figcaption>
</figure>
</details>

## Routing a keystroke

In the workstation split, if the agent pane is active, press `Ctrl-b`, then Left Arrow to select the shell. At the left-hand prompt, type a lowercase `x` without pressing Enter. The arrow you used to select the shell and the letter you typed beside its prompt reached tmux through the same outer terminal. A **key binding** associates a key with an action in tmux. The server handled the arrow after the prefix as a request to select the pane to the left. With the default bindings, a plain `x` has no tmux action, so the server forwards it to the active pane.

At attach, tmux sets the outer terminal to deliver keys as you type and turns off its **echo**, the operating system's option to send a copy of your input back to the screen. Follow the lowercase `x` through the split. Your desktop app writes the character to the outer master. The server reads it at the other end and writes it to the left pane's master. At this point the shell has a letter to work with and the desktop still needs an instruction to draw it. [tmux terminal setup](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/tty.c)

At a Bash prompt, **Readline** is the part of Bash that lets you edit a command before you run it. It reads individual keys with the pane terminal's echo turned off. Readline adds the `x` to the command you're editing and writes the letter to the pane terminal. Other shells, including zsh, have their own line editors. tmux reads the output from the pane master and puts the letter in the shell's grid. You see it when the server draws that cell. The agent gets no part of the exchange. It can send output to its own grid while you're partway through a command on the left. [GNU Readline terminal management](https://tiswww.case.edu/php/chet/readline/readline.html#Terminal-Management)

<figure>

![Steps 1 to 4 take x from the keyboard to Bash through the active pane's PTY. In step 5 Readline writes x to the pane. In step 6 tmux updates the grid and redraws the letter.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-keypress-roundtrip.png)

<figcaption>Read the top row from left to right, then the bottom row from right to left. In step 5, “bash echoes x” means that Readline writes x to the pane. The character has crossed the pane PTY twice by step 6, once toward Bash as input and once back toward tmux as output. The one letter beside the prompt came back from the program you typed it into.</figcaption>
</figure>

## Scrollback belongs to the pane

At the left-hand prompt, press Backspace to erase the `x`. The line editor removes the letter and tmux updates that cell. Now press Enter at the empty prompt until the timestamp scrolls off the top. Each press makes the shell print a fresh prompt on another line. You can fill the left pane with prompts without scrolling a line of the agent's output on the right. Each pane has its own **scrollback**, a history of lines that have scrolled off its **main screen**, the screen where you enter shell commands and read their results.

Keep the left pane selected and press `Ctrl-b`, then `[` to enter **copy mode**. tmux now takes your navigation keys so you can browse the lines it has kept for this pane and select text to copy. Press Up until the timestamp comes into view, or use Page Up to move a page at a time (Fn and Up Arrow on a Mac keyboard without a Page Up key). You're reading a line that scrolled off the shell's screen, in a desktop window that didn't exist when the shell printed it. You can follow the line to the edge of the pane without picking up part of the agent's output in the neighboring column.

You can recover the timestamp because `date` wrote one line and left it there. Imagine a clock in its place that prints `10:42`, moves the cursor back to the last digit a minute later and writes a `3` over the `2`. That operation changes one cell, like the X in the diagram. If the line scrolls off the top after the change, you'll find `10:43` in copy mode. To find both readings in the history, you'd need the clock to have written them on separate lines.

`history-limit` sets how many scrolled-off lines tmux keeps per pane. Once the server discards a line to make room, you can no longer page back to it. If a test prints enough output on the right to reach that limit, you can lose the beginning of its report there and still find your timestamp in the history on the left.

In the left pane, press `Ctrl-c` to leave copy mode. You should see the live prompt where you left it. [tmux default bindings](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/master/key-bindings.c)

A full-screen program such as Vim can request an **alternate screen**, a temporary screen for its interface. If you were editing a file in Vim, you could page through it without pushing each page into the shell's scrollback. To find an earlier line of the file, you scroll in the editor. If you detach mid-edit, tmux can redraw the editor at your current place when you return. After you quit Vim, you see the shell screen that was there before you opened the file. In either screen the program arranges its text to fit the dimensions tmux gives the pane.

## One pane width for two clients

Two people looking at the same pane can have different-sized desktop windows. The shell has one terminal from which to read its width, so tmux has to choose how much space to give it. You can measure that width as a second viewer joins the session and again after they leave. Widen your current desktop window. At the left-hand prompt in that window, set the rule for fitting the split to its clients, then measure the space the shell has now.

```sh
tmux set-option -w window-size smallest
stty size
```

The `-w` sets the option for this tmux window. With `smallest`, tmux gives it a width and height that fit both clients once the second one attaches. The two numbers from `stty size` give the shell pane's rows and columns, in that order. Write down the second number. It measures the space available to the shell between the left edge and the divider. The agent has its own share of the window on the other side.

Leave the wide client attached. On the same machine and account, open a second desktop terminal about the same height but narrower. Place it where you can see both. At its prompt outside tmux, run `tmux attach -t agent`. At the left-hand prompt in the narrow client, run `stty size` again. Watch the command appear in both windows as you type, followed by the same result with a smaller column count. Both clients display the output from the same shell.

Look at the space past the right edge of the split in the wide terminal while the narrow client is attached. For a 120-column client and an 80-column client, the whole tmux window fits into 80 columns under the `smallest` rule. The shell pane gets part of those 80 after tmux allows room for the agent and the divider. Both viewers see a long command wrap at the same place. The extra columns on the wide desktop fall outside the shared layout, so the shell has no use for them when it draws the prompt. [tmux window sizing](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1#window-size)

On attachment the server reads the new terminal's dimensions. If you resize an attached desktop window, its client reports the change over the control socket. The server applies the sizing rule, adjusts the split and sets the new dimensions on the affected pane PTYs. The operating system tells the foreground program in each resized terminal that its size has changed. The shell can read its new width and redraw the line you're editing. The agent can fit its interface to the space on the other side.

Under the default settings the clients share the active pane as well. In the narrow client, press `Ctrl-b`, then Right Arrow. Keep your keyboard focus in the narrow desktop terminal and watch the wide one. Its active-pane indicator moves to the agent too. In the narrow client, press `Ctrl-b`, then Left Arrow to select the shell again. If someone were typing in the wide client, your choice of pane would change where their next letter went. [tmux windows and panes](https://man.openbsd.org/tmux.1#WINDOWS_AND_PANES)

In the narrow client, press `Ctrl-b`, then `d` to **detach**. This disconnects that client from the session and returns you to the shell where you ran the attach command. The wide client stays attached. At its left-hand prompt, run `stty size` once more. With the narrow client gone, the pane has room to grow back to the width you wrote down. You ran all three measurements in the same shell. Typing `exit` at its prompt would end that shell and, with the default settings, close its pane.

## What survives an SSH disconnect?

If you're staying on one machine, you can keep working in the desktop client. For the laptop handoff, detach your remaining workstation client. With that desktop terminal in focus, press `Ctrl-b`, then `d`. You'll return to the shell outside tmux. The server still holds both pane terminals on the workstation, ready for a new connection from another login under the same account.

Open a terminal on your laptop. At the laptop prompt, run `ssh user@workstation`, using the workstation's hostname and the same account you used to create the session. SSH gives you a shell on the workstation. At that remote prompt, outside tmux, run the attach command.

```sh
tmux attach -t agent
```

You start the client at the workstation prompt because that's where the session lives. The name `agent` identifies a session on a tmux server under your workstation account. The client you started over SSH can reach that server over the same local control socket your desktop client used. An attach command at the laptop's own prompt would look for a server on the laptop. After the remote attach, you have the workstation split in front of you. [OpenSSH manual](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh.1#INTERACTIVE_SESSIONS)

<p id="answer-the-agent">If you came here to answer the agent, press <code>Ctrl-b</code>, then Right Arrow to select its pane and give it the test command. Your answer reaches the agent through the terminal where it asked the question, <code>/dev/pts/5</code> in the drawing. If the agent starts the tests, they run in the project on the workstation. You can read the results at your laptop.</p>

Your laptop terminal app holds the master of its local PTY, as the workstation's desktop app did in the first drawing. The `ssh` program runs on the other end and carries your input to a separate **login PTY** on the workstation. The workstation's SSH service holds the master of that login PTY. Your remote shell uses its program-facing end. At attach, the remote tmux client passes a handle for that end to the tmux server. For this client, the login PTY serves as the outer terminal.

Follow a line of test output back to the laptop. The tmux server reads the line at the pane master and updates the grid for the agent. It writes the corresponding screen changes to the login PTY. The workstation's SSH service reads those updates from the master and sends them over the network to `ssh` on the laptop. Your terminal app draws the changes in the right pane. The pane terminal and the server that holds its master have stayed on the same side of the network throughout.

Close the laptop terminal while it shows the split. Once SSH detects the disconnect, the old login terminal hangs up and its tmux client can exit. The pane terminals belong to the server on the workstation. It can keep reading their output as long as the workstation and server stay up. Rebooting that machine would end the session. To reconnect after the dropped login, open a new terminal on the laptop. Run the same SSH command at its prompt and, once you have a fresh workstation prompt, run `tmux attach -t agent` there.

<figure>

![A lost SSH connection ends the old login PTY and client. After the next login, solid arrows carry keys and screen updates between the laptop, the new login PTY and the running tmux server. The new client hands over its terminal on a dashed socket. The pane PTYs and programs remain on the remote machine.](../../assets/blog/how-terminal-multiplexers-work/mux-ssh-drop.png)

<figcaption>Start at the lower laptop. The solid green path runs over SSH to the new login PTY and from there to the tmux server. Keys go toward the server and screen updates come back along the same path. The dashed arrow from the new tmux client shows the socket it uses to pass the terminal handle at attach. On the right, the server still holds the pane PTYs for the programs you left running. The crossed-out client above belonged to the old login.</figcaption>
</figure>

In the reattached split on the laptop, look for the active pane. If the agent pane is active, press `Ctrl-b`, then Left Arrow to select the shell. At the left-hand prompt, run `tty`. In the diagram's example you'll read `/dev/pts/4`, the terminal you measured on the workstation before you split the window. Both SSH logins gave you access to that shell. Losing the first login cost you the view through its outer terminal. The tmux server kept the terminal the shell was using, along with the agent's terminal on the other side of the divider. You can type your next command at the prompt where you left off.
